From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 17 Aug 2026 20:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] Assert host interrupts on INTF.ARM, with a guest-owned
 acknowledge

Moves platform interrupts off INTF.IPL and onto the channel that was always
meant to carry them. 0002 put them on INTF.IPL because upstream's INTF.ARM
lifecycle is coupled to Paula at both ends -- armed only when the guest has
INTEN+EXTER in the INTENA shadow, cleared only by a guest write to INTREQ --
and neither end exists on a machine with no chipset. That reasoning was about
the lifecycle, never about the channel.

The channel itself needs nothing. ExecutionLoop.c maps INTF.ARM to level 6
unconditionally, ahead of any chipset consideration:

    if (ctx->INTF.ARM)      level = 6;
    else if (ctx->INTF.PPC) level = 2;

What was missing is an acknowledge that does not go through INTREQ, and Emu68
already ships one: MOVEC on the JITCTRL2 control register (0x1e0) exposes
INTF.ARM as bit 29 -- readable as pending state, write-1-to-clear
(M68k_LINE4.c:2104 for the write, :2365 for the read). Register to register,
no MMIO, no fault, nothing to emulate. That is docs/New_emu68.md section 18's
M68kClearHostInterrupt(), present in stock Emu68 and never used because
nothing wrote the byte.

So this patch is only the assert half: store into INTF.ARM instead of
INTF.IPL, still with no INTENA test and no shadow. The deassert lives in the
guest, at aros/arch/m68k-emu68/platform/platform.c, and the two have to land
together -- INTF.ARM is a latch, so an unacknowledged one re-enters level 6
the moment the guest's RTE drops the SR mask.

The value stored is 1, not 6. The byte is only ever tested for truthiness by
the arbitration (the level is a constant 6 by contract), but JITCTRL2 exposes
bit 0 of it, so storing 6 makes the register read back "nothing pending" --
6 & 1 == 0. That is AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0001, an upstream defect this
change has to fix in order to work at all.

What this leaves behind: INTF.IPL goes back to meaning what it means
everywhere else -- a level decided by something that resolves an Amiga IPL,
which here will be Rigel. Two domains, neither depending on the other, as
docs/New_emu68.md sections 3 and 4 lay out.

patches/emu68/0001 stays. It is what makes INTF.IPL mean anything on a
standalone build, and INTF.IPL is now exclusively the Amiga domain's -- which
is the separation docs/New_emu68.md sections 3 and 4 ask for. Nothing here is
a reason to remove it.

What is *not* settled is whether 0001 has the right shape for that domain,
and the two design documents disagree:

  * docs/New_emu68.md section 15 draws a push -- Rigel resolves the level and
    writes INTF.IPL, which is the PiStorm32 branch 0001 enables.
  * docs/Rigel_integration.md sections 25 and 26 specify a pull --
    rigel_get_ipl() exposes the current level, "queried after Rigel
    advancement", which is the shape of the PISTORM_CLASSIC branch 0001
    routes standalone builds away from.

The pull shape has the better argument. Rigel holds a level derived from
INTENA & INTREQ rather than delivering an event, and Rigel_integration.md
section 28 is normative that accepting the exception MUST NOT clear the
source -- so 0001's auto-clear of INTF.IPL, correct here where nothing would
ever lower it, is wrong there. Section 29 then requires the effective level
to be re-evaluated whenever either domain changes, which a latched field does
not give you and a query does.

Note it cannot be a pure pull either way: the arbitration only runs when
INT64 != 0, so something must still set a byte in INTF to open that gate.
Flag to open the gate, query for the authoritative level -- which is exactly
what PISTORM_CLASSIC does, and is a decomposition rather than a quirk.

None of that has to be decided to land this patch. It is recorded because
this is the patch that hands INTF.IPL over to the Amiga domain, and the next
person to touch it should not have to rediscover that the two documents point
in different directions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/aarch64/vectors.c | 20 +++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/aarch64/vectors.c b/src/aarch64/vectors.c
--- a/src/aarch64/vectors.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/vectors.c
@@ -156,13 +156,15 @@
 "       mrs x0, SPSR_EL1                \n" // Get SPSR
 "       orr x0, x0, #0x080              \n" // Disable IRQ interrupt so that we are not disturbed on return
 "       msr SPSR_EL1, x0                \n"
-// Hand the CPU a level through INTF.IPL, the same field an external
-// interrupt controller writes on PiStorm. No INTENA test and no shadow:
+// Assert the host interrupt on INTF.ARM, the channel the arbitration
+// already maps to level 6 unconditionally. No INTENA test and no shadow:
 // with no Paula in the machine there is nothing for a guest to arm, and
-// no register access has to fault to deliver an interrupt.
+// no register access has to fault to deliver an interrupt. The guest
+// deasserts through MOVEC on JITCTRL2 bit 29, which is why the value
+// stored is 1 and not the level -- bit 0 is what that register exposes.
 "       mov x1, "CTX_POINTER_ASM"       \n" // Load CPU context
-"       mov w0, #6                      \n" // Set level 6 IRQ
-"       strb w0, [x1, #%[ipl]]          \n"
+"       mov w0, #1                      \n" // Assert host interrupt
+"       strb w0, [x1, #%[pint]]         \n"
 "       ldp x0, x1, [sp], #16           \n" // Restore scratch registers
 "       eret                            \n"
 "2:     ldp x0, x1, [sp], #16           \n"
@@ -177,10 +179,10 @@
 "       mrs x0, SPSR_EL1                \n" // Get SPSR
 "       orr x0, x0, #0x0c0              \n" // Disable IRQ and FIQ interrupts so that we are not disturbed on return
 "       msr SPSR_EL1, x0                \n"
-// Same as the IRQ path above: a level into INTF.IPL, no shadow.
+// Same as the IRQ path above: assert INTF.ARM, no shadow.
 "       mov x1, "CTX_POINTER_ASM"       \n" // Load CPU context
-"       mov w0, #6                      \n" // Set level 6 IRQ
-"       strb w0, [x1, #%[ipl]]          \n"
+"       mov w0, #1                      \n" // Assert host interrupt
+"       strb w0, [x1, #%[pint]]         \n"
 "       ldp x0, x1, [sp], #16           \n" // Restore scratch registers
 "       eret                            \n"
 "                                       \n"
